ster high species\ndiversification. Beyond their ecological value, the landscapes of the Pilcomayo form part of the ancestral\nterritories of the Guaraní, Tapiete, and Weenhayek peoples, as well as long-established Creole populations,\nwhose cultural traditions and livelihoods are intimately tied to the health of the river. Safeguarding the\nPilcomayo is therefore essential not only for biodiversity and climate resilience, but also for the well-being\nand cultural heritage of the communities who depend on it.\nTHREATS\nOverfishing, unsustainable livestock ranching, wildfires and deforestation.\n1 VERB/ACTION Executive SummaryAm nonsequi\ndolutatem reperit TasRperAsp CeleKnd aRe EconCsedO uRt mDagnita\nconsedicitia audignatquo et iume nus et ad equibusBeris\nNATIVA\nExecutive SummaryAm nonsequi dolutatem reperit aspersp elendae consed ut\n• mFoagrn 1it5a cyoenasresd,i cNitiaat aivuad ighnaast qwuoor ekt eiudm teo gnuetsh eet ra dw eiotsh a sl oecuaml iauns,d q uinibtuesrFnuagtaio. nal stakeholders within the\nFaccum quo quature pellitibus con con nonsequia alit, intem. Ut porro bla\ntrinational Pilcomayo River Basin.\nsunt porunt qui re dolorer fersperspe delignis quatem quas sus a cor solut fuga.\n• Advanced community-based enterprises such as beekeeping and sustainable livestock ranching,\nimproving income opportunities while reducing pressure on natural resources.\n• Developed infrastructure and promoted practices that enhance community resilience to drought,\nincluding improved access to water.\n• Expanded community access to appropriate technologies\n• Strengthened Indigenous territorial governance with an emphasis on meaningful participation of\nwomen, ensuring equity and long-term stewardship.\n• Consolidated its presence in the basin through an operational base in Villamontes and the\nestablishment of the Palo Santo and Napichán Private Reserves.\n47 47\nActivities\nAnnex VII Pilcomayo Ark\n3-YEAR OBJECTIVES\n1 VERB/ACTION Executive SummaryAm nonsequi\ndoluta