 quas sus a cor solut fuga.\nCentral and southern Paraguay, along the Paraguay, Paraná and Pilcomayo rivers, centered on\nAguape’y Reserve (8,624 hectares) and Mbaracayú Forest Nature Reserve (64.406 hectares).\nSTATUS\nFoundation\nECOSYSTEM TYPE\nWetlands and forest\n50 50\nActivities\nAnnex VIII Paraguayan Action Plan\nCONSERVATION VALUE\n1 VERB/ACTION Executive SummaryAm nonsequi\ndolutatem reperit aspersp elendae consed ut magnita\nThe confluence of the Paraguay, Paraná, and Pilcomayo rivers forms a critical ecological corridor in South\nconsedicitia audignatquo et iume nus et ad equibusBeris\nAmerica, linking vast wetlands and forest ecosystems across international borders. These riparian landscapes\nsustain extraordinary biodiversity, serving as vital dispersal routes for wide-ranging species such as the jaguar,\ngiant oEtxteerc,u ltoivwe lSaunmdm taapryirA, man ndo mnsaerqsuhi ddeoelurt. aTtehme rreipveerrist aaslspoe rpspro evleidned ahea cbointaste dfo urt threatened and migratory\nmagnita consedicitia audignatquo et iume nus et ad eos as eum ius, quibusFuga.\nspecies, including red-and-green macaws, wood storks, and large freshwater fish like dorado and sábalo,\nFaccum quo quature pellitibus con con nonsequia alit, intem. Ut porro bla\nwhich are essential for both ecosystem health and local food security.\nsunt porunt qui re dolorer fersperspe delignis quatem quas sus a cor solut fuga.\nThis corridor is crucial for connectivity between fragmented populations from the Pantanal, Chaco, and\nAtlantic Forest, to enable gene flow, buffer ecosystems against the effects of climate change, and ensure the\nsurvival of endangered species while safeguarding water, carbon storage, and livelihoods for Indigenous\nand local communities downstream. Seasonal floods replenish soils, sustain aquatic food webs, and maintain\nsome of the richest wetlands on the continent. Beyond their ecological importance, these waterways are\ncultural and economic lifelines for Indigenous and local communities who depend on them for fishing,\ntransportation, and agriculture.\nTHREATS\nExtensive livestock ranching, uncontrolled fires, poaching and retaliatory killing of jaguars.\n1 VERB/ACTION Executive SummaryAm nonsequi\ndolutatem reperit aspersp elendae consed ut magnita\nTRACK RECORD\nconsedicitia audignatquo et iume nus et ad equibusBeris\nExecutive SummaryAm nonsequi dolutatem reperit aspersp elendae consed ut\nFUNDACIÓN MOISÉS BERTONI\nmagnita consedicitia audignatquo et iume nus et ad eos as eum ius, quibusFuga.\nFaccum quo quature pellitibus con con nonsequia alit, intem. Ut porro bla\n•sunFt pMorBu nhta qs uail rree addoylo irdeern fetrifispeedr slpaer gdee lpigontiesn qtuiaatle amre qausa tso s curse aa ctoe rs tseoplupti fnugg as.t ones. A detailed assessment\nwill be carried out to determine ecological value, land type, property size, and ownership,\nbefore inviting landowners to sign collaboration agreements.\n• The 1,300-hectare Aguape’y Reserve was recently donated to FMB.\n• The FMB team is monitoring jaguar populations in Mbaracayú Forest Nature Reserve.\n51 51\nActivities\nAnnex VIII Paraguayan Action Plan\n3-YEAR OBJECTIVES\n1 VERB/ACTION Executive SummaryAm nonsequi\ndolutatem reperit aspersp elendae consed ut magnita\nEXPANDco nPseRdOiciTtiEa CauTdIigOnNatquo et iume nus et ad equibusBeris\n• Initiate the acquisition process of the first property to establish the first Ark.\nExecutive SummaryAm nonsequi dolutatem reperit aspersp elendae consed ut\nmag•nitAa scsoenssse dpirciiotirai tayu dsiigtneast qtuoo c erte iautme es nteups peti nadg esotso anse esu aml oiunsg, qtuhieb uPsFauragga.u ay, Paraná and Pilcomayo\nFaccumri vqeuros .quature pellitibus con con nonsequia alit, intem. Ut porro bla\nsunt porunt qui re dolorer fersperspe delignis quatem quas sus a cor solut fuga.\n• Sign agreements with private landowners for the creation of stepping stones.\nREDUCE THREATS\nStrengthen enforcement through:\n• Strengthen protection by hiri