ón de la Naturaleza, Universidad de Chile,\nSantiago, Chile\n3Corporación Nacional Forestal, región de Aysén, Chile\nCorrespondence: Cristián Saucedo (cris tian. sauce do@re wildi ngchi le.or g) and Benito A. González (bengonza@uchile.cl)\nWildlife Biology The presence of humans and large carnivores in shared landscapes presents unique\nchallenges for wildlife conservation and management. This study explores the tempo-\n2025: e01396\nral activity patterns of tourists and pumas Puma concolor using camera traps to under-\ndoi: 10.1002/wlb3.01396\nstand the potential for encounters between the two groups in Patagonia National Park,\nSubject Editor: Nuria Selva Chile. The park, characterized by a cold temperate climate with diverse wildlife and\nEditor-in-Chief: Ilse Storch vegetation, spans over 304 527 hectares. Data collected from August 2020 to March\nAccepted 13 February 2025 2022 reveal distinct activity patterns for pumas and tourists, with pumas being primar-\nily nocturnal and tourists being more active during the day. Despite a low overall over-\nlap coefficient (Dhat = 0.27), specific periods during twilight hours showed increased\ninteraction potential. Seasonal variations revealed a significant increase in overlap dur-\ning the autumn of 2021, with stable overlap patterns observed both before and after\nthis period. Seasonal variations in activity patterns highlight the need for adaptive\nmanagement strategies for human–puma interactions. These findings underscore the\nimportance of enforcing park regulations, promoting responsible tourism practices,\nand implementing specific behavioral protocols to ensure the safety of both tourists\nand pumas in protected areas. This study provides valuable insights for enhancing con-\nservation efforts in the face of increasing wildlife-watching tourism worldwide.\nKeywords: camera traps, human–wildlife interactions, Patagonia, pumas, tourism,\nwildlife\nIntroduction\nCohabitation with humans sometimes promotes tolerance of wildlife to human activi-\nties (Bejder et al. 2009, Lowry et al. 2013, Frank 2016). Nevertheless, cohabitation\nand casual encounters might generate potential conflicts between wildlife and humans\n© 2025 The Author(s). Wildlife Biology published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of Nordic\nSociety Oikos\nwww.wildlifebiology.org This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons\nAttribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any\nmedium, provided the original work is properly cited. Page 1 of 9\n(Mekonen 2020, Oommen 2021, Somu and Palanisamy patterns could help to understand the potential of encounters\n2022). Encounters between wildlife and humans occur across between tourists and pumas, which is key to ensure the safety\nall aspects of the urban-to-natural gradient in various regions of both and the implementation of better management prac-\nworldwide (Shepherd et al. 2014, Gebo et al. 2022, Montero- tices in conservation areas.\nBotey and Perea 2023). Interactions involving wild carnivores\nare particularly concerning because human life is at potential\nrisk of injuries or predation (Bombieri et al. 2018). When Methods\nhumans enter the habitat of large carnivores for recreational\npurposes, during activities such as hiking or camping, casual Study area\nencounters with them become common. Likewise, the rise of\nThe study area is located in the Patagonia National Park\nwildlife-watching tourism has made these encounters explic-\nitly desirable for many (Marion et al. 2020). Human inter-\n(PNP), 46°55ʹS 72°12ʹW, Aysén district, Chile, with the\nresearch conducted within the Chacabuco Valley sector\nest in documenting events for recreational or professional\n(Fig. 1). This national park was established in 2018 by com-\npurposes has experienced a sharp increase in recent decades\nbining the donation of the Chacabuco Valley ranch, adjoining\n(Howlett et al. 2023, Von Essen et al. 2023). This form of\nlands (83 73