id': 'e2100d76-ccde-4165-a8d2-7995378b37cc', 'drive_file_id': '1nGj6ERn5EX1iD9LlYshnadeYYZQicbDs', 'name': 'African-Parks.pdf', 'mime_type': 'application/pdf', 'is_folder': False, 'web_view_link': 'https://drive.google.com/file/d/1nGj6ERn5EX1iD9LlYshnadeYYZQicbDs/view?usp=drivesdk', 'modified_time': datetime.datetime(2026, 6, 30, 15, 57, 33, tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc), 'text_content': 'Matusadona, 1992\n.\nThe\nAfrican context\nAfrica is home to the world’s largest intact landscapes and\nhosts 25% of the planet’s biodiversity\nThe\nAfrican context\n3x\nUnited Nations/Statista 2023\nThe Sahel: 1990\nThe Sahel: 2020\nThe Sahel: 1990\nThe Sahel: 2020\nThe Sahel: Today\n“What is not being managed today, will be lost tomorrow”\nIvory Coast Ivory Coast\nDeforestation\nHuman footprint\nLiberia Liberia\nOur\nPortfolio\n• 24 parks\n• 13 countries\n• 20m ha\nKundelungu\n• $200m budget (2026)\n• 6,250 employees (97% local)\n• 500k people inside areas we manage\nThe\nModel\n1 Mandates\nBiodiversity Conservation\n2 Management Community Development\nRevenue Generation\n3 Money\nInfrastructure,\ngovernance, safeguards\n1 Mandates\n1 Mandates: Governance Structures\nGovernment (Sovereignty)\nMacro • Owner, legislation, statutory oversight\nPark Board (Gov, AP, key stakeholders)\n• Strategic oversight, approves plans &\nMeso\nbudgets\nAfrican Parks (Management)\n• Management within defined framework\nMicro\n2 Management:\nBiodiversity Conservation\n>2,200\nRangers\n37 11,610\nSpecies Animals\nResearch\n40+\npartnerships\n.\n2 Management:\nCommunity Development\nCommunities\n280,000\nengaged\nEnvironmental\n34,000\nEducation visits\nRevenue shared\n$4.4m\n/made\n.\n2 Management:\nRevenue Generation\n188,000 59% Local\nTourists\ntourists\n$16m 111\nRevenue Lodges\n2 Management: Enabling functions\n3 Money\nOverhead\n9%\ncosts\n$180\n$160\n$140\n$120\n$100\n$80\n$60\n$40\n$20\n$0\n2006 2007 2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 2025\nInternal reserves Donor contributions RPeavrekn rueev eannude cost recoveries\nCarbon & VNU Endowment income\nThe\nChallenges\n2030 Strategy Overview\nConservation Development\nNOISIV\n30%\nMandates Management Money\n0302\nLANOITUTITSNI\nEVITCEJBO\nMROFTALP\nTo have the greatest conservation impact in Africa\n30 PAs, 30m ha Portfolio implementing 3 Funding: $180m\n10 Incubators pillars = Ecological, Social, Endowment: $1b\nFinancial Sustainability NBS: $35m\nSafeguards\nOperations Marketing & Communications\nCommunity Development Human Resources\nFunding Finance\nOur\nValues\nThe\nLessons learned\n• Clarity of purpose / focus – relentless on execution\n• Build off success (e.g. Chinko)\n1800 kms\nDevelopment of\nthe Chinko Core Zone\n500k ha 3m ha\nSudan\nLand Use Plan participatively\nChad developed over 20m ha\n9.5m ha formally decreed for\ncommunity custodianship\nSouth\nSudan\nCentral African\nRepublic\nDRC\nThe\nLessons learned\n• Clarity of purpose and remain focused\n• Build off strength (e.g. Chinko)\n• Results create the opportunity for funding\n• Be careful of mediocrity (detail matters)\n• Humility to listen to others, but be discerning in your response\n• Set the plan locally – and fund it\n• Recruit for values “keeping the soul”\n• Scaling requires the institutional platform', 'description': None, 'parent_drive_file_id': '1j30dn9xdw7s4AKhjPXrWfnh8vkAwHQAg', 'children': []}, {'id': '55345451-80fa-4aa1-b2ef-518614ef822d', 'drive_file_id': '1e_rPR2onTvVxxYYn87Xu4_Z-BGBzVxrV', 'name': 'Landscapes-of-Hope-American-Prairie-by-Daniel.pdf', 'mime_type': 'application/pdf', 'is_folder': False, 'web_view_link': 'https://drive.google.com/file/d/1e_rPR2onTvVxxYYn87Xu4_Z-BGBzVxrV/view?usp=drivesdk', 'modified_time': datetime.datetime(2026, 6, 30, 16, 1, 51, tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc), 'text_content': 'Rewilding the Space Between\nWilderness and Working Lands', 'description': None, 'parent_drive_file_id': '1j30dn9xdw7s4AKhjPXrWfnh8vkAwHQAg', 'children': []}, {'id': 'a3c908c4-c273-4ebe-9af3-0f6912d1cd1d', 'drive_file_id': '1XeIW_jsWtxlRwoGSpLsASHL7emDmU7Je', 'name': 'Landscapes-of-Hope-Leuser-by-Kevin-LCF.pdf', 'mime_type': 'application/pdf', 'is_folder': False, 'web_view_link': 'https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XeIW_jsWtxlRwoGSpLsASHL7emDmU7Je/view?usp=drivesdk', 'modified_time': datetime.datetime(2026, 6, 30, 16, 1, 51, tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc), 'text_content': "PROTECTING THE MAGICAL LEUSER\nECOSYSTEM, INDONESIA\nLEUSER CONSERVATION FORUM (FKL)\nhttps://leuser.or.id\nEcological services for 4 million people\n@paulhiltonphoto\nOne of the highest biodiversity hotspots in\nthe world\n• 382 bird species\n• 174 mammal species\n• 192 reptile species\n• 52 amphibian species\n• > 4500 plant species\nDeforestation\nIllegal Logging\nHabitat fragmentation\nWildlife\npoaching and\nhuman-wildlife\nconflict\nDeforestation impacts on community\n54 yrs = 429,224 ha loss\n20% (Destroyed)\n80% (Magical Forest)\nABOUT\nFKL\nThe Leuser Conservation Forum (Forum\nKonservasi Leuser; FKL) is recognized as\none of the leading organizations\noperating today to protect and restore\nthe Leuser Ecosystem in Aceh - with the\nlargest coverage on state forest and the\nlargest wildlife protection teams spread\nacross any one landscape in Indonesia.\nOUR VISION\nAND MISSION\nVision Mission\nThe conservation of the •\nLeuser Ecosystem that\n•\nbenefits the community in\n•\na sustainable manner\n•\n•\n4 450 $4,000,000\nOFFICES STAFF ANNUAL EXPENDITURES\n2013 2025\nCOLLABORATION WITH\nGOVERNMENT PARTNERS\nMINISTRY OF KPH / ACEH FORESTRY\nFORESTRY AGENCY\nREPUBLIC OF PROVINCIAL\nINDONESIA GOVERNMENT OF\nACEH\nCOLLABORATION WITH NGOs, INSTITUTIONS AND UNIVERSITIES\nygetartS\nLKF\nProtect Restore Develop\nFKL safeguards wildlife Through both passive and FKL fosters long-term\nthrough dedicated active restoration conservation by raising\nprotection teams for approaches, FKL public awareness via\ngeneral fauna and rehabilitates degraded conservation outreach and\nelephants, operates rapid- landscapes—promoting educational programs, and\nresponse rescue units, natural forest regeneration by empowering\nmaintains strategic guard while implementing communities with\nposts, and manages a agroforestry systems to sustainable livelihoods.\nSumatran rhino sanctuary accelerate forest recovery\nto secure critical habitats and enhance community\nand deter poaching. resilience.\nWILDLIFE\nPROTECTION\nTEAM\nPATROL\nPEOPLE PER\nEXPEDITION\nTEAM\nFOR\nThe Leuser Conservation Forum Rangers Team\nis the front guard in fighting threats to 4-5 38 8 15\nbiodiversity in the Leuser ecosystem.\nThese brave individuals embark on rigorous\nexpeditions into the wilderness to protect key\nwildlife habitats, dismantle snares, and collect INCLUDING\nRANGER GUARD DAYS\nwildlife presence data, demonstrating an GOVERNMENT\nTEAMS POSTS\nunparalleled commitment to conservation. RANGER\nwww.leuser.or.id\nLEUSER\nRESCUE\nTEAM\nCurrently, FKL operates 4 Elephant Protection Teams, 1\nElephant Fencing Team and 1 Elephant Biobarrier Team,\nand 1 Wildlife Rescue Unit. These units are spread\nthroughout the Leuser Ecosystem and are effective in\nreducing human-wildlife conflict and conflict-related\nwildlife deaths in the Leuser Ecosystem.\nwww.leuser.or.id\nMOBILE\nMONITORING\nUNITS\nwww.leuserconservancy.or.id\nSumatran Rhino Sanctuary under construction\nBOMA PADDOCK\nMATERNITY ENCLOSURE\nSRS OFFICE\nPROTECT\n38 Wildlife Protection Teams\n8 Guard Posts\n4 Elephant Patrol Teams\n1 Elephant Fencing Team\n1 Elephant Biobarrier Team\n1 Wildlife Rescue Team\n1 Sumatran Rhino Sanctuary\n16 Mobile Monitoring Units\nRESTORATION\nMANAGES INVOLVES RESTORATION AREA\n24\n818 12,000\nFKL Restoration Team mission is to\nrestore degraded forests and support\ncommunity livelihoods through various\nrestoration approaches, namely RESTORATION AGROFORESTRY HECTARES\nagroforestry and natural regeneration.\nPOSTS FARMERS\nRESTORE\n24 Restoration Posts\n12,000 Hectares\nRESEARCH AND\nOPERATES\nMONITORING STATION\n4\nFKL manages 4 research stations, namely the Ketambe,\nSoraya, Samarkilang, and Sarah Baru Research Station.\nRESEARCH AND\nThe research station functions as an education center\nfor students conducting research in the Leuser MONITORING\nEcosystem, and a monitoring post for forest guard STATIONS\nteams.\nwww.leuser.or.id\nCONSERVATION\nREACHING ORGANIZING VISITING\nOUTREACH 10 80\n> 4,300\nFKL operates 1 education unit to conduct\nconservation education and outreach to school SCHOOL FIELD TRIPS TO SCHOOLS\nstudents and communities in the Leuser STUDENTS AND LEUSER\nEcosystem.\nCOMMUNITIES ECOSYSTEM\nSUSTAINABLE\nLIVELIHOODS\nDeveloping community-based MANAGES SUPPORTING ESTABLISHED ESTABLISHED\neconomic alternatives—such as\n82 2 2\nagroforestry, organic farming, and >12,000\ndeforestation-free cocoa and palm\noil farms—to ensure local people\ncan thrive without harming the\nFARMER FARMERS SUSTAINABLE SUSTAINABLE\necosystem.\nGROUPS PRODUCTION AGRICULTURE\nCENTERS Of TRAINING\nEXCELLENCE CENTER\nDEVELOP\n4 Research Stations\n1 Conservation Education and\nOutreach Unit\n12,000 Sustainable Smallholder\nFarmers\nConservation outcomes\nImprovement of wildlife population through protection\n0,06\n0,055\n0,05\n0,04\n0,038\n0,038\n0,03\n0,02\n0,018\n0,015\n0,012\n0,011 0,011 0,011\n0,01\n0\n2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 2025\nMK/tinU\nRatio of Wildlife Snare Found per Km Patrol in Leuser Ecosystem\n(2017-2025)\nYear\n0,045\n0,041\n0,04\n0,035\n0,03\n0,025\n0,020\n0,02\n0,017\n0,016\n0,015\n0,013\n0,011\n0,010\n0,009\n0,01\n0,008\n0,005\n0\n2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 2025\nMK/tinU\nRatio of Forest Encroachment (case per km patrol)\n2017-2025\nYear\n0,05\n0,045\n0,044\n0,045\n0,04 0,039\n0,038\n0,034\n0,035\n0,032\n0,031\n0,03 0,029\n0,025\n0,022\n0,02\n0,015\n0,01\n0,005\n0\n2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 2025\nMK/tinU\nRatio of Illegal Logging Case Found per Kilometer Patrol\n2017-2025\nYear\n0,3\n0,268 0,26